1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,712
The school outline joins reasoning, respiratory support and medication
delivery in the same week. The goal is to manage a changing patient,
not to finish one device demonstration and mentally start over at the
next table.

2
00:00:14,712 --> 00:00:28,416
Use the earlier Week Two lessons as preparation: three A for
reasoning, one C for consent and capacity, seven B for respiratory
assessment, and eight A and eight B for pharmacology and delivery.

3
00:00:28,416 --> 00:00:42,312
Here, original cases combine those parts and introduce a change after
your first action. For every intervention, state the expected effect,
the time for reassessment and the finding that would make you change
course.

4
00:00:42,312 --> 00:00:53,400
The school medication-math gate remains your own assessed task. The
practice in this lesson does not access it, answer it, submit it or
certify that you have passed.

5
00:00:53,400 --> 00:01:05,376
You find a thirty-one-year-old adult with a definite pulse, three
shallow breaths per minute and pinpoint pupils. A bystander says the
person may have used a sedative, but cannot identify it.

6
00:01:05,376 --> 00:01:20,592
The immediate deficit is inadequate ventilation. Position, clear and
support the airway, using two-provider BVM when available. A
medication history is useful, but cannot be allowed to postpone an
effective breath.

7
00:01:20,592 --> 00:01:34,344
Reduced consciousness, depressed breathing and miosis support
suspected opioid toxicity, but none excludes a concurrent problem.
Check glucose and look for injury or other causes while support
continues.

8
00:01:34,344 --> 00:01:46,968
If the pulse becomes absent or cannot be definitely identified
promptly, move to the arrest pathway. Do not continue a pulse-present
medication scenario after the patient has changed categories.

9
00:01:46,968 --> 00:01:59,664
State clearly who owns ongoing ventilation. A frequent error is that
everyone turns toward the drug kit, leaving a poorly breathing patient
unsupported while the correct medication is being prepared.

10
00:01:59,664 --> 00:02:13,752
With limited personnel, choose the safest workable division and
request help early. Prepare equipment within reach, use closed-loop
requests and avoid repeatedly handing the mask away without a
replacement seal.

11
00:02:13,752 --> 00:02:28,344
Name the patient and medication out loud, read the actual
concentration and check the intended amount independently. An
independent check means recalculating from the source information, not
agreeing with a number already suggested.

12
00:02:28,344 --> 00:02:41,232
As soon as delivery is complete, return attention to breathing,
oxygenation and circulation. Record the exact time and actual dose so
that repeat decisions are based on a real timeline.

13
00:02:41,232 --> 00:02:52,008
The treatment target is adequate breathing, not a demand that the
patient immediately become fully awake. Continue supportive
ventilation while naloxone has time to act.

14
00:02:52,008 --> 00:03:09,552
The checked adult IM or IV sequence is zero point four, zero point
four, zero point eight and then two milligrams, with the specified
three-minute reassessment intervals when the response is inadequate.
This is not four identical zero-point-four doses.

15
00:03:09,552 --> 00:03:24,648
Dose is not volume. A simulated zero-point-four-milligram amount
requires zero point four millilitres from one milligram per
millilitre, but one millilitre from zero point four milligrams per
millilitre. Read the actual label every time.

16
00:03:24,648 --> 00:03:39,552
For intranasal use, distinguish the authorized syringe-atomizer
procedure from a sealed fixed-dose spray. Do not add atomizer dead-
space calculations to a sealed product, and do not delay breathing
support while choosing a route.

17
00:03:39,552 --> 00:03:52,200
After treatment, the patient opens the eyes briefly but still takes
only shallow ineffective breaths. That is not the required respiratory
endpoint. Reassess the airway and maintain support.

18
00:03:52,200 --> 00:04:05,496
Compare rate, depth, chest movement, oxygenation and capnography where
appropriate. A rising respiratory count with almost no tidal movement
can still be inadequate ventilation.

19
00:04:05,496 --> 00:04:18,000
Watch for vomiting, withdrawal, distress and new respiratory
difficulty, and respond calmly. Do not describe every confused or
frightened response as deliberate non-cooperation.

20
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:33,696
Persistent impaired consciousness may reflect co-intoxication, head
injury, glucose disturbance or another illness. Repeat assessment
instead of escalating medication indefinitely to force a preferred
level of alertness.

21
00:04:33,696 --> 00:04:45,096
The antagonist response and the opioid exposure may have different
durations. A patient who is breathing adequately now can become
sedated and hypoventilate again.

22
00:04:45,096 --> 00:04:55,704
Continue respiratory and consciousness checks and keep working support
available. Do not pack away the bag and suction merely because the
patient has spoken once.

23
00:04:55,704 --> 00:05:08,640
Record the timeline of each dose, effect and recurrence, together with
possible long-acting or mixed exposure. That timeline helps the
receiving team distinguish improvement from temporary masking.

24
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:22,968
Current J twelve directs use of the actual refusal-of-care pathway
when transport is declined. Do not replace it with a remembered older
treat-and-release checklist or an automatic rule that everyone who
wakes is safe to leave.

25
00:05:22,968 --> 00:05:39,552
An unresponsive patient cannot give the same informed decision as an
alert cooperative patient. Identify and document the applicable
emergency authority while providing necessary care; the phrase implied
consent is not a complete legal analysis.

26
00:05:39,552 --> 00:05:53,064
When the person improves, reassess the capacity to make the particular
decision and explain the recommended next steps. Earlier emergency
authority does not grant indefinite control over a now-capable person.

27
00:05:53,064 --> 00:06:06,936
Discuss recurrence, incomplete reversal, co-intoxication and
recommended assessment in understandable language. Check understanding
rather than simply asking for a signature or a yes to a leading
question.

28
00:06:06,936 --> 00:06:21,072
Use the current consultation and refusal procedure for the actual
situation. Substance use does not itself prove incapacity, and
standing up or knowing the date does not itself prove capacity for the
relevant risk decision.

29
00:06:21,072 --> 00:06:35,064
A seventy-two-year-old adult is awake, breathing thirty-two times per
minute and speaking only two or three words. There is marked effort,
bilateral crackles and low oxygen saturation despite oxygen.

30
00:06:35,064 --> 00:06:48,648
The pattern may fit cardiogenic pulmonary edema, but crackles alone
are not a diagnosis. Assess the history, airway, circulation and
important alternatives while selecting immediate support.

31
00:06:48,648 --> 00:07:02,736
Unlike the opioid case, this person is generating frequent spontaneous
breaths and can follow commands. Determine whether airway integrity,
cooperation and the current CPAP criteria are actually present.

32
00:07:02,736 --> 00:07:14,520
Prepare a rescue plan before applying positive pressure. A treatment
that initially helps can become unsuitable if consciousness, blood
pressure or ventilatory effort deteriorates.

33
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:24,888
Use the full current PR zero nine selection and exclusion criteria
from the respiratory lesson. A low saturation alone is not enough to
choose CPAP.

34
00:07:24,888 --> 00:07:36,600
CPAP maintains positive pressure while the patient generates breaths.
It does not produce the required ventilation for apnea or replace
airway protection in an obtunded vomiting patient.

35
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:51,000
Check the actual training requirement, assess circulation and prepare
the device at its specified oxygen flow and pressure. A flow setting
copied from another system may produce a different pressure or oxygen
concentration.

36
00:07:51,000 --> 00:08:01,200
Explain the mask and coach the patient. Start monitoring before
application so that later improvement or hypotension can be compared
with a real baseline.

37
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:12,984
After application, ask the patient whether breathing feels easier and
observe whether work and speech improve. Recheck chest movement,
respiratory rate and air entry.

38
00:08:12,984 --> 00:08:25,872
Compare saturation with its target and baseline, while recognizing
that equipment and inspired oxygen change with the system. A single
transient number does not replace reassessment of the patient.

39
00:08:25,872 --> 00:08:39,096
Repeat blood pressure and perfusion checks because positive pressure
can reduce venous return. A patient who now looks pale, weak and less
responsive may be deteriorating even if saturation rises.

40
00:08:39,096 --> 00:08:52,944
Document the actual device settings, tolerance and response, and keep
rescue ventilation available. A trial is an ongoing conditional
treatment, not a decision that must be defended after new
contraindications appear.

41
00:08:52,944 --> 00:09:04,896
Now the same patient becomes drowsy, stops following commands and has
weak irregular respirations. Do not continue CPAP simply because it
was appropriate several minutes earlier.

42
00:09:04,896 --> 00:09:18,024
Reassess airway integrity, breathing and circulation immediately.
Announce the deterioration and transition to the support required now,
including effective BVM when ventilation is failing.

43
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:30,864
Prepare suction and the appropriate adjunct, establish crew roles and
request advanced help. Removing one mask without promptly establishing
the next effective support is not a completed rescue plan.

44
00:09:30,864 --> 00:09:45,360
Report the before-and-after findings and time relation to treatment.
The cause may be progression, exhaustion, pressure-related compromise
or another process; the first job is to support the failing function
while investigating.

45
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:57,168
A capnogram has a shape, respiratory rate and end-tidal value. Read
all three with breathing mechanics and circulation rather than turning
the number into a diagnosis.

46
00:09:57,168 --> 00:10:11,016
A sloped shark-fin pattern suggests obstructed expiration, but needs
correlation with air movement, wheeze, history and the device. A
normal-looking shape does not exclude significant illness.

47
00:10:11,016 --> 00:10:25,824
A suddenly low or missing trace can represent a disconnected sampler,
leak, displaced airway, absent ventilation or poor perfusion. Look at
and support the patient immediately, then trace the sampling path.

48
00:10:25,824 --> 00:10:41,208
In practice, ask the instructor to change one variable at a time. Say
which observation distinguishes an equipment fault from true patient
deterioration, and demonstrate that you would not withhold care while
waiting for a perfect trace.

49
00:10:41,208 --> 00:11:06,816
The current Week Two Moodle outline lists six procedural rights:
patient, medication, dose, route, time and documentation. Its four
clinical reasoning rights are reason, consent, integrity and response.
Use those exact current outline labels when preparing this weekend.
This is the school page wording, not a claim that a statute contains
this numbered list.

50
00:11:06,816 --> 00:11:32,064
The saved Eight B assistance lesson instead names the right to refuse
and expiry with integrity, while some other saved sections use eight
rights. These are real source differences. The next slide identifies
that older wording; it does not replace the current outline. Valid
consent includes a decision-specific assessment and respect for a
capable refusal, not simply obtaining a signature.

51
00:11:32,064 --> 00:11:58,848
Translate the current list into evidence. Confirm two patient
identifiers, read the actual product and concentration, establish the
indication and authority, calculate the amount and volume, and check
the actual route and interval. Inspect expiry and solution integrity.
Explain the proposed care and confirm the person can make this
decision or identify the applicable emergency authority.

52
00:11:58,848 --> 00:12:25,272
After actual delivery, record what was given and when, then compare
breathing, circulation, symptoms and adverse effects with the
baseline. That is the right response, not a promise to reassess later.
These checks join Saturday airway and capnography work to Sunday IM,
SC and IV push preparation. The real school medication math gate
remains your own assessed task.

53
00:12:25,272 --> 00:12:43,512
The school assistance section names ten rights: patient, medication,
dose, route, time, documentation, reason, refusal, expiry and
integrity, and response. Other saved sections use eight; do not
pretend all lists are identical.

54
00:12:43,512 --> 00:12:58,608
Translate each word into an observable action. For the right dose,
show the source amount, actual concentration, calculation, syringe
volume and maximum check; saying right dose aloud is not a
verification.

55
00:12:58,608 --> 00:13:12,648
Confirm previous doses, allergies, contraindications and the actual
treatment authority even if those checks are not separate numbered
rights in this particular list. The list is a memory aid, not a
ceiling on assessment.

56
00:13:12,648 --> 00:13:26,448
The right response requires examining whether the intended benefit
occurred and whether harm appeared. Complete documentation after
actual delivery, never as a promise that an unadministered dose has
already been given.

57
00:13:26,448 --> 00:13:40,488
Start by writing what is required and what the product contains.
Convert milligrams and micrograms before dividing, and carry the units
so that the final answer becomes millilitres rather than an
unexplained number.

58
00:13:40,488 --> 00:13:52,608
For a fictional one-point-five-milligram amount with two milligrams
per millilitre, divide one point five by two to obtain zero point
seven five millilitres. The milligrams cancel.

59
00:13:52,608 --> 00:14:06,864
Then ask whether the amount meets the ordered or protocol maximum, the
route volume limit, the required speed and the syringe graduations. An
arithmetically correct result can still be impossible or unsafe to
deliver.

60
00:14:06,864 --> 00:14:20,736
Use a leading zero for values below one and avoid unnecessary trailing
zeros. Recalculate independently, estimate the expected size and stop
if the label, source or result does not agree.

61
00:14:20,736 --> 00:14:37,176
If a calculation asks for two hundred fifty micrograms but the vial is
labelled zero point five milligrams per millilitre, first recognize
that zero point five milligrams equals five hundred micrograms. The
volume is zero point five millilitres.

62
00:14:37,176 --> 00:14:50,232
For gravity flow, volume times drop factor divided by time in minutes
gives drops per minute. Hours in a minutes formula can create a
sixtyfold error even when the multiplication is correct.

63
00:14:50,232 --> 00:15:01,968
For weight-based treatment, calculate the required amount, apply any
maximum and only then convert to volume. Do not apply a milligram
maximum to the millilitre result.

64
00:15:01,968 --> 00:15:15,000
If a result does not fit the route or device, resolve it rather than
rounding to a convenient mark or assuming the school intended a
different concentration. The final problem set provides original
examples for this check.

65
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:26,736
A simulated kit stores similar-looking concentrations together, the
reference card is outdated and the preparer is interrupted. Those are
latent conditions that make an error easier.

66
00:15:26,736 --> 00:15:40,368
The student then reads one vial but uses another concentration in the
calculation. That is the active failure. Telling the student to be
careful addresses neither the misleading storage nor the obsolete
reference.

67
00:15:40,368 --> 00:15:53,496
An independent product-and-unit check catches the mismatch before
delivery. Stop the uncertain medication, keep needed patient support
going and resolve the discrepancy using a reliable current source.

68
00:15:53,496 --> 00:16:09,168
Afterward, report the near miss and improve the system. If a wrong
dose was actually administered, immediate patient assessment,
appropriate help, honest communication and accurate reporting take
priority over hiding the event.

69
00:16:09,168 --> 00:16:22,128
For IM and SC, name the target tissue and demonstrate the actual
school landmarks and device selection under supervision. A remembered
angle alone is not a complete injection technique.

70
00:16:22,128 --> 00:16:37,368
For IN, verify the actual formulation and device. For inhaled therapy,
observe the interface and patient technique; an actuation count or
visible mist alone does not establish the delivered dose.

71
00:16:37,368 --> 00:16:53,256
For IV push, trace the line to the correct patient, verify the site
and patency, prepare the connector and deliver at the specific drug
rate. Account for residual drug in the line without flushing it as an
unintended rapid dose.

72
00:16:53,256 --> 00:17:06,576
After each route, inspect the patient and the delivery path,
especially after movement. Record incomplete or failed delivery
accurately instead of equating the amount prepared with the amount
administered.

73
00:17:06,576 --> 00:17:19,488
At station A, the observer introduces secretions, a seal problem or
device movement. You must recognize ineffective ventilation and
recover it, not just finish insertion steps.

74
00:17:19,488 --> 00:17:33,408
At station B, begin with an appropriate CPAP candidate and then
receive a new finding such as hypotension or reduced awareness. State
whether the original decision still holds and demonstrate the new
support plan.

75
00:17:33,408 --> 00:17:48,768
At station C, distinguish an obstructive pattern from an absent
sampling trace, using the patient rather than a memorized picture
alone. At station D, identify a concentration mismatch before a
supervised route task.

76
00:17:48,768 --> 00:18:01,800
Rotate leader, airway, medication and observer roles. These are
original preparation stations aligned to the saved outline; the real
school rubric and required sign-offs still control assessment.

77
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:11,976
Begin with the actual baseline and current condition rather than the
name of the device you used. Explain the reason for treatment and the
evidence that the problem was urgent.

78
00:18:11,976 --> 00:18:23,592
Report actual settings, doses, route and time, including any failed
delivery or treatment stopped for harm. Do not omit a problem because
it was corrected before arrival.

79
00:18:23,592 --> 00:18:37,152
Compare the response with the effect you expected. For the opioid
case, explain current spontaneous ventilation and recurrence risk; for
CPAP, explain work of breathing, circulation and tolerance.

80
00:18:37,152 --> 00:18:51,024
End with the unresolved priority and any relevant consent or
consultation issue. A handover is a transfer of responsibility and
clinical uncertainty, not merely a recitation of normal numbers.

81
00:18:51,024 --> 00:19:04,152
First separate observation from inference. Four shallow breaths per
minute is an observation; opioid toxicity is a working explanation
that still needs response-based and differential reassessment.

82
00:19:04,152 --> 00:19:18,024
Explain the decisive branch rather than narrating every hand movement.
For example, reduced awareness with ineffective breathing changed the
support from a spontaneously triggered mask strategy to assisted
ventilation.

83
00:19:18,024 --> 00:19:29,376
Compare intended and actual effects and identify anything that
worsened. Invite the observer to identify a missed cue or an
assumption that was never tested.

84
00:19:29,376 --> 00:19:43,008
Choose one reproducible improvement, such as reading the concentration
before arithmetic or rechecking the mask after every move. Repeat the
changed step and verify that it actually fixes the observed problem.

85
00:19:43,008 --> 00:19:55,176
Finish the original practice without looking at the worked solutions,
then explain each error in units and clinical meaning. Repeating only
the questions you remember is not a reliable check.

86
00:19:55,176 --> 00:20:07,944
Bring unresolved source differences and device questions to the
instructor before the corresponding assessed task. Do not resolve
conflicting versions by quietly choosing the one that is easier to
memorize.

87
00:20:07,944 --> 00:20:19,128
The saved outline requires passing the medication-math gate before
progressing. This course does not know the actual current cut score or
retest policy and does not invent either.

88
00:20:19,128 --> 00:20:30,240
After preparation, you complete the official assessment yourself. Keep
the school requirement, your demonstrated skill and this independent
teaching package as three distinct records.
